# Mechanics 21 — The tennis-racket theorem: a free rigid body spun about its intermediate axis
#
# (Goldstein, Classical Mechanics, §5.6–5.7; Landau & Lifshitz, Mechanics, §37 (problems);
#  Taylor §10.8–10.9.)
# A phone, modelled as a uniform box m = 0.200 kg, 15.0 cm × 7.50 cm × 0.800 cm, is
# tossed spinning. In body axes along the 15, 7.5 and 0.8 cm edges the principal
# moments are I₁ = m(b² + c²)/12 < I₂ = m(a² + c²)/12 < I₃ = m(a² + b²)/12, and with
# no torque Euler's equations read
#     I₁ ω₁' = (I₂ − I₃) ω₂ ω₃,   I₂ ω₂' = (I₃ − I₁) ω₃ ω₁,   I₃ ω₃' = (I₁ − I₂) ω₁ ω₂.
#
#   (a) Linear stability: for a spin ω₀ about axis i, a small wobble obeys
#       δ'' = −(I_i − I_j)(I_i − I_k)/(I_j I_k) ω₀² δ. Give the wobble frequencies
#       about axes 1 and 3 and the growth rate about axis 2, for ω₀ = 10.0 rad/s.
#   (b) Spin about axis 2 with a tiny wobble: ω(0) = <0, 10.0, 0.100> rad/s.
#       Integrate Euler's equations (as one vector ODE) for 20 s. Check that the
#       kinetic energy E and |L| stay constant.
#   (c) The body flips over: ω₂ changes sign again and again. Landau's exact solution
#       (for L² > 2EI₂) has ω₂ ∝ sn(τ, k), τ = t √((I₃ − I₂)(L² − 2EI₁)/(I₁I₂I₃)),
#       k² = (I₂ − I₁)(2EI₃ − L²) / ((I₃ − I₂)(L² − 2EI₁)). Starting at ω₁ = 0 means
#       τ(0) = K(k), so ω₂ first vanishes after a time K/rate and then every 2K/rate,
#       with K(k) = ∫₀^{π/2} dθ / √(1 − k² sin²θ). Compare with the ODE.
#   (d) The same wobble about axes 1 and 3 stays small: the largest excursion of the
#       two small components over 20 s.

m = 0.200 kg
a = 15.0 cm
b = 7.50 cm
c = 0.800 cm
I1 = m (b² + c²) / 12
I2 = m (a² + c²) / 12
I3 = m (a² + b²) / 12
ω0 = 10.0 rad/s
print "principal moments:", I1 to 6 digits, I2 to 6 digits, I3 to 6 digits

# (a) linear stability
print "wobble frequency about axis 1:", ω0 √((I2 - I1) (I3 - I1) / (I2 I3)) to 6 digits
print "wobble frequency about axis 3:", ω0 √((I3 - I1) (I3 - I2) / (I1 I2)) to 6 digits
print "growth rate about axis 2:", ω0 √((I2 - I1) (I3 - I2) / (I1 I3)) to 6 digits

# (b) Euler's equations as a vector ODE
euler(w) = <(I2 - I3) / I1 * w.y w.z, (I3 - I1) / I2 * w.z w.x, (I1 - I2) / I3 * w.x w.y>
solve ω' = euler(ω)
  with ω(0) = <0, 10.0, 0.100> rad/s
  for t from 0 s to 20 s tolerance 1e-12
energy(w) = ½ (I1 w.x² + I2 w.y² + I3 w.z²)
angmom(w) = √((I1 w.x)² + (I2 w.y)² + (I3 w.z)²)
E = energy(ω(0 s))
L = angmom(ω(0 s))
print "kinetic energy:", E to 8 digits, " after 20 s:", energy(ω(20 s)) to 8 digits
print "|L|:", L to 8 digits, " after 20 s:", angmom(ω(20 s)) to 8 digits
print "ω at 5 s:", ω(5 s)

# (c) the flips against Landau's elliptic-function solution
rate = √((I3 - I2) (L² - 2 E I1) / (I1 I2 I3))
k2 = (I2 - I1) (2 E I3 - L²) / ((I3 - I2) (L² - 2 E I1))
K_ell = ∫ 1 / √(1 - k2 sin(θ)²) dθ from 0 to π/2
print "k²:", k2 to 10 digits, " K(k):", K_ell to 8 digits
w2(t) = ω(t).y
solve w2(t_flip1) = 0 rad/s for t_flip1 from 0 s to 20 s
solve w2(t_flip2) = 0 rad/s for t_flip2 from t_flip1 + 0.01 s to 20 s
print "first flip, ODE vs Landau:", t_flip1 to 7 digits, (K_ell / rate) to 7 digits
print "time between flips, ODE vs Landau:", (t_flip2 - t_flip1) to 7 digits, (2 K_ell / rate) to 7 digits

# (d) the stable axes
solve p' = euler(p)
  with p(0) = <10.0, 0.100, 0.100> rad/s
  for t from 0 s to 20 s tolerance 1e-12
solve q' = euler(q)
  with q(0) = <0.100, 0.100, 10.0> rad/s
  for t from 0 s to 20 s tolerance 1e-12
big1 = 0 rad/s
big3 = 0 rad/s
for tt from 0 s to 20 s step 0.01 s
    big1 = max(big1, abs(p(tt).y), abs(p(tt).z))
    big3 = max(big3, abs(q(tt).x), abs(q(tt).y))
print "largest wobble, spin about axis 1:", big1 to 4 digits
print "largest wobble, spin about axis 3:", big3 to 4 digits
